      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} - 4 \\cos{(x )}&=- 4 y \\sin{(x )} + 4 \\\\ 4 \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 4 y \\sin{(x )}&=4 \\cos{(x )} + 4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(\\sin{(x )})}_{P(x)}y&=\\underbrace{\\cos{(x )} + 1}_{Q(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\sin{(x )}$ and $Q(x)=\\cos{(x )} + 1.$",
